From 9771d640e4bcd2af80ec774918bc622c4bfeb1db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TeamHarrisCali <34009957+TeamHarrisCali@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:10:18 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Wi-Fi: fix the RF type 3 channel table walk

The baseband entry count is the byte before the table (as dswifi reads it),
not the RF entry count + 1. On the DSi (2 baseband, 2 RF entries) the old
loop wrote RF entry 0 into baseband register 1, never set RF register 1
(coarse frequency) and wrote 0xFF to RF register 0xFF: the radio never
tuned to the chosen channel.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 linux-2.6.x/arch/arm/mach-nds/arm7/wifi.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/linux-2.6.x/arch/arm/mach-nds/arm7/wifi.c b/linux-2.6.x/arch/arm/mach-nds/arm7/wifi.c
index 63dd0de8f..cdb4cad11 100644
--- a/linux-2.6.x/arch/arm/mach-nds/arm7/wifi.c
+++ b/linux-2.6.x/arch/arm/mach-nds/arm7/wifi.c
@@ -316,9 +316,16 @@ static void Wifi_SetChannel(int channel)
 		return;
 
 	if (ReadFlashByte(0x40) == 3) {
+		/* The firmware's channel table: after the 0x42 initial RF bytes at 0xCE comes a count of baseband entries, then those
+		 * entries, then 0x43 RF entries; each entry is a register number and 14 per-channel values. The original loop took the RF
+		 * count + 1 as the baseband count: on this DSi (2 and 2) it wrote RF entry 0 into baseband register 1, never set RF
+		 * register 1 (the coarse frequency) and wrote 0xFF into RF register 0xFF, so the radio never tuned to the chosen channel. */
+		int nbb;
+
 		n=ReadFlashByte(0x42);
 		n+=0xCF;
-		for(i=0;i<=ReadFlashByte(0x43);i++) {
+		nbb = ReadFlashByte(n - 1);
+		for(i=0;i<nbb;i++) {
 			Wifi_BBWrite(ReadFlashByte(n),ReadFlashByte(n+channel+1));
 			n+=15;
 		}
-- 
